1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:06,160
Okay, so most of the model calls in an agent harness aren't actually writing anything.

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:07,680
They're making decisions.

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:16,960
They're making decisions like which tool should I use here, what skill should I load, is this command safe to run, and even decisions for things like RAG,

4
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:21,840
where it's asking which of these 20 search results actually answers the question.

5
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:28,320
Up until recently, everybody has approached those decisions pretty much in the same way.

6
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:31,920
They've used a full LLM call to get the answer back.

7
00:00:32,206 --> 00:00:40,720
So in this video, I want to show how you could use JEV and the other open decision models to actually improve an agent harness from the inside.

8
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:43,440
I'm going to talk about some of the things that you can actually do with it.

9
00:00:43,630 --> 00:00:51,204
I'll show you a couple of cool examples that I've been playing with for doing progressive disclosure on lots of skills and for doing re-ranking of RAG results.

10
00:00:51,758 --> 00:00:58,340
But I'll also talk about some general principles and perhaps more importantly, what are the use cases that you don't want to use a model like this?

11
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:02,560
All right, so let's start with the loop that every agent runs.

12
00:01:02,894 --> 00:01:05,120
A request comes in and it goes to an LLM.

13
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:14,800
Along with that, you basically have some kind of tool registry going in there, a system prompt, perhaps a set of skills, some guidelines of how the agent's going to work, etc.

14
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:20,240
And the LLM decides straight away what action should it take, whether it needs to use a tool.

15
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:26,240
And if it does, it writes out a function call, giving us the tool name plus the arguments as JSON.

16
00:01:26,606 --> 00:01:30,640
Your scaffold in your agent harness then runs that code as a function.

17
00:01:30,958 --> 00:01:33,920
It gets the results and it appends that to the conversation.

18
00:01:34,094 --> 00:01:36,400
And the whole thing goes back to the LLM.

19
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:43,620
And then round and round this goes until eventually your LLM's decided it's done and it writes its final answer.

20
00:01:43,982 --> 00:01:53,360
Along that road, it's probably going to use many tools, it's basically going to get information to expand its context, and it's going to make a lot of decisions along the way.

21
00:01:53,678 --> 00:02:01,680
Now, if you look at those LLM steps to see what's actually going on at each step, the whole sort of thing of asking itself which tool it should use,

22
00:02:01,934 --> 00:02:03,588
is it safe to actually run that?

23
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:13,200
And often because it's making those decisions itself, it never really stops to ask any sort of verification step or any kind of safety step in there to ask,

24
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:14,720
is this safe to run?

25
00:02:14,958 --> 00:02:25,280
And for most agents, if they don't have any sort of reflective step in there, they're really never even asking themselves, is this result good enough or should I go around again and try again?

26
00:02:25,582 --> 00:02:35,268
Each of those things are clear decisions that you can use a decision model like JEV or one of the open GEVs to actually improve the quality of the agent harness.

27
00:02:35,694 --> 00:02:42,400
On top of that, a lot of the times the decisions that the LLM is actually making are just a small set of options.

28
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:48,772
Do I go to this node next or this node? Which skill looks like the best one to use in this situation?

29
00:02:49,166 --> 00:02:52,720
Which skill do I ask for more information in this situation?

30
00:02:53,038 --> 00:02:57,360
And each time you're paying for a full call to a large LLM there.

31
00:02:57,646 --> 00:03:01,600
And that's costing you both in money for tokens, but often even more.

32
00:03:01,774 --> 00:03:05,860
It's costing you in time of you actually having to go through and do these things.

33
00:03:06,350 --> 00:03:14,640
And that's why a lot of the time when people are building custom agents, they often don't put anywhere near as many verification steps in there as they should

34
00:03:14,990 --> 00:03:17,828
simply because that's just going to slow everything down.

35
00:03:18,190 --> 00:03:26,276
So if you've watched any of my other JEV videos, you know that I've basically talked about the whole concept of JEV as being like a smart if statement.

36
00:03:26,734 --> 00:03:37,280
You give it a state which is just the thing that you're evaluating, and then you give it a set of typed questions about that state, and it gives you back typed answers with probabilities.

37
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:38,400
No text at all.

38
00:03:38,976 --> 00:03:41,252
Choice will pick one option from a list.

39
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:44,772
Score rates the state on a scale that you define.

40
00:03:45,038 --> 00:03:49,696
A null is a yes or no or a true or false that's returned back as a probability.

41
00:03:50,094 --> 00:04:00,176
And the cool thing is you're not limited to one question at a time. You can ask many questions about the one state all in a single call and get answers back in parallel.

42
00:04:00,302 --> 00:04:03,456
And it almost doesn't change the response time at all.

43
00:04:03,726 --> 00:04:11,776
So if you're looking at how to improve your agent harness, one of the key things you can do is look at that loop of where it's full

44
00:04:11,776 --> 00:04:19,396
of a bunch of if statements that are dressed up as function tools or dressed up as asking the model for structured JSON responses back.

45
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:24,016
And you can convert those kind of calls to something that's much quicker, like a decision model.

46
00:04:24,302 --> 00:04:27,044
All right, so where does this actually belong in your agent harness?

47
00:04:27,502 --> 00:04:30,884
I'd say that there's six main places that you want to be thinking about here.

48
00:04:31,278 --> 00:04:36,256
So the first one is model routing. I did a whole video about how you can look at this.

49
00:04:36,416 --> 00:04:44,336
You've got a request coming in, Jev decides whether it goes to a cheap, fast model, or expensive model that's going to do much better reasoning, etc.

50
00:04:44,782 --> 00:04:49,572
If you haven't watched the video that I made about that, you can see the whole thinking process for actually putting that together.

51
00:04:49,838 --> 00:04:52,036
The second use is risk gating.

52
00:04:52,334 --> 00:05:01,412
So before a tool call actually runs, Jev can actually check whether it's going to be something destructive like deleting a database or wiping a folder,

53
00:05:01,646 --> 00:05:04,496
or whether what it's actually going to be doing is quite benign.

54
00:05:04,750 --> 00:05:11,056
Now we know that a lot of the coding agents have had this kind of check for a while, but it's been hidden generally in their harness.

55
00:05:11,502 --> 00:05:17,296
And when you were using it, you were basically paying for a full tool call with the amount of tokens that it actually gets used there.

56
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:24,016
So being able to configure your harness so that when certain tools get called, perhaps like a bash tool,

57
00:05:24,270 --> 00:05:32,896
you actually look at what the arguments are going to be going into that and you send that to Jev to basically ask it, hey, is this going to be malicious or benign?

58
00:05:33,296 --> 00:05:36,416
Very quickly, you've got a safety check that can take milliseconds.

59
00:05:36,718 --> 00:05:40,576
And of course, with the open decision models, that can be running fully locally as well.

60
00:05:40,942 --> 00:05:43,376
Third up, you've got tool and skill selection.

61
00:05:43,662 --> 00:05:51,044
So generally, what people have done is they inject their entire tool registry that they're going to be using into the system prompt.

62
00:05:51,374 --> 00:05:53,540
And that can chew up thousands of different tokens.

63
00:05:53,902 --> 00:05:58,816
So the whole idea of skills has reduced that a lot by using progressive disclosure.

64
00:05:59,056 --> 00:06:05,892
This is one of the key things that I'm going to show you in the demos a bit, that you can actually have lots of skills going on, categorize them,

65
00:06:06,176 --> 00:06:14,256
have your decision model decide which kind of category it's going to need, and then just load the appropriate skills into a system prompt at that point.

66
00:06:14,574 --> 00:06:17,284
Fourth up, you've got things like ranking and judging.

67
00:06:17,614 --> 00:06:19,616
So this is another one that I want to sort of demo here.

68
00:06:19,918 --> 00:06:30,736
I think the whole idea of scoring retrieved chunks and grading on answers against a rubric, this can be a huge win for anything that you're doing with RAG or anything you're doing

69
00:06:30,926 --> 00:06:33,696
where you're injecting context into the context window.

70
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:44,656
Fifth up, we've got things like triage. So this is all your things like support tickets, email issues, and one call here can give a proper probability of how urgent something is

71
00:06:44,942 --> 00:06:48,496
and start to help giving things an actual probability of priority here.

72
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:55,216
And this one can be really useful for when to basically get the model to go back to the human for things like human in the loop as well.

73
00:06:55,598 --> 00:07:00,336
And the sixth use that I'm not going to go into too much here is the whole idea of just real-time filtering.

74
00:07:00,558 --> 00:07:08,416
This can be really useful for any time you're basically dealing with APIs, polling requests, anything where it's good to just filter what

75
00:07:08,416 --> 00:07:12,416
comes back from that before you inject that into the context window.

76
00:07:12,686 --> 00:07:17,060
So the rule of thumb that I use for all of these actually comes from TypeSafe themselves.

77
00:07:17,456 --> 00:07:26,336
If a panel of smart people could answer the question in just a few seconds, then it's the kind of question that you probably should be using a decision model for.

78
00:07:26,606 --> 00:07:33,252
If they need to go away and formulate an answer and then write a complicated answer back, that's when you should be sticking with the LLMs.

79
00:07:33,614 --> 00:07:39,044
In a little bit, I'll actually go through the use cases of where you shouldn't be using these decision models at all.

80
00:07:39,310 --> 00:07:41,412
So how do you actually wire this in?

81
00:07:41,902 --> 00:07:47,140
So if we go back to that loop that we had there, there are four places to hook a decision in.

82
00:07:47,534 --> 00:07:52,736
And really they're going to be the same in any framework, whether you're talking about Langchain, whether you're talking about ADK,

83
00:07:52,816 --> 00:07:56,656
whether you're just using custom Python code to write your own loop here.

84
00:07:56,942 --> 00:08:01,636
You've got before the prompt is built, so decide which skills or tools to load.

85
00:08:02,094 --> 00:08:05,796
Before the model call, decide which actual model you're going to call.

86
00:08:06,350 --> 00:08:09,296
Before a tool runs, decide whether it's safe.

87
00:08:09,966 --> 00:08:16,816
And then after a result comes back, decide whether it's good enough to use or whether you should go back and actually go through the loop again.

88
00:08:17,134 --> 00:08:19,776
So each hook here is the same small piece of code.

89
00:08:20,174 --> 00:08:31,296
Build a state from what the loop already has, ask your questions, and then read the probabilities and answers that come back, and then branch or make your decision for the path going forward

90
00:08:31,502 --> 00:08:32,388
based on those.

91
00:08:32,718 --> 00:08:38,564
Now there's also a second pattern that you can use where JEV is the loop's decider and the LLM is the fallback.

92
00:08:38,736 --> 00:08:45,700
So JEV picks the tool or the answer and only hands it to the LLM when it's unsure or when something needs text to be written.

93
00:08:46,190 --> 00:08:51,056
Now this pattern can actually cut more LLM calls, but it's definitely a much bigger change to your agent.

94
00:08:51,342 --> 00:08:56,976
And so far I found with this one it just doesn't seem to work as well when you've got a lot more sort of complicated things going on in there.

95
00:08:57,230 --> 00:09:00,324
Anyway, for either of these patterns, you don't need a framework.

96
00:09:00,590 --> 00:09:08,736
That said, the first pattern that I talked about here, Langchain has already shipped that as middleware, and Pydantic has shipped their own version by making the TypeSafe

97
00:09:08,896 --> 00:09:12,336
models something that you can just plug in and use in the agent loop.

98
00:09:12,526 --> 00:09:17,060
All right, so before I show you the demos, let me just go through quickly where I think this doesn't fit.

99
00:09:17,376 --> 00:09:29,220
Clearly, anything where you need text back, anything that needs any sort of multi-step reasoning, and anything where you're going to need to connect several things together to generate an answer out,

100
00:09:29,616 --> 00:09:32,096
all of those are still an LLM's job.

101
00:09:32,302 --> 00:09:41,604
And in many ways, a lot of that multi-hop stuff is still going to be the stuff that you're going to use the LLM for and then have your harness process that and keep passing it back to the large LLM.

102
00:09:41,902 --> 00:09:44,708
The second thing you want to think about here is size.

103
00:09:45,166 --> 00:09:55,216
So if your state is just getting too big and that you're trying to pass in a hundred thousand tokens to get some kind of response back from that context window,

104
00:09:55,376 --> 00:09:57,296
then JEV is not going to be a good fit here.

105
00:09:57,550 --> 00:10:01,956
TypeSafe's own docs say that accuracy drops a lot on long inputs.

106
00:10:02,446 --> 00:10:09,376
And so it generally seems to fit whether you're using the TypeSafe JEV decision model or one of the open decision models.

107
00:10:09,582 --> 00:10:18,496
On top of that, another thing obviously is that JEV itself doesn't use images. Now, some of the open models actually do use images and use images really well.

108
00:10:18,702 --> 00:10:23,300
So at the moment, that's definitely a win for the open decision models over the proprietary models.

109
00:10:23,694 --> 00:10:26,372
All right, another thing that doesn't work well are compound questions.

110
00:10:26,926 --> 00:10:30,724
So compound questions are often something that the decision models don't handle that well.

111
00:10:31,118 --> 00:10:33,536
If you've got a yes-no question, okay, that's fine.

112
00:10:33,616 --> 00:10:41,476
But if you've got something that requires multiple questions in there to be answered, then often the LLM is going to do better for that kind of thing.

113
00:10:41,806 --> 00:10:51,056
Another thing you want to be wary about in here is while the decision models can be really good at spotting sort of weak spots, you want to be careful about how you always rely on them.

114
00:10:51,342 --> 00:10:59,536
It does seem currently that a lot of these models can be prompt injected to basically have instructions that actually move the results out of that.

115
00:10:59,822 --> 00:11:08,896
So often the way to get around that is to basically have a question first that asks, are there any prompt injections in here before you actually pass it on to the real question that you want

116
00:11:09,056 --> 00:11:09,668
to actually ask?

117
00:11:10,126 --> 00:11:15,172
The other thing I find too is that you really want to think about criteria for making the decisions.

118
00:11:15,598 --> 00:11:26,016
So if you've got very clear decision spots where you're asking the same questions, you can control the actual criteria very well in situations like that.

119
00:11:26,256 --> 00:11:33,216
If you're just asking some vague question about the output from the LLM and you haven't got a nice clear criteria in there,

120
00:11:33,582 --> 00:11:38,736
I wouldn't necessarily be trusting the LLM to write that in a way that's going to make the whole system a lot better.

121
00:11:38,958 --> 00:11:46,736
You kind of want to define the instructions and the criteria yourself and then benchmark any changes that you make to that over time.

122
00:11:47,054 --> 00:11:55,748
And then, of course, lastly, if you're actually running your agent locally, but then you're using JEV in the cloud, you've got to think about the whole fact that it's a hosted API.

123
00:11:55,950 --> 00:11:59,696
And do you really trust it for every state that you're going to send to the cloud?

124
00:11:59,918 --> 00:12:01,828
Is it okay that that leaves your machine?

125
00:12:02,094 --> 00:12:06,016
And I think this is where the open decision models have really come along so far now.

126
00:12:06,318 --> 00:12:14,916
So let's jump into the demos and actually look at some examples of how you could do this for a couple of these key tasks and some of the challenges that you might face along the way.

127
00:12:15,630 --> 00:12:21,648
All right, so in the demo here, I've basically made a UI just so you can see what's going on a lot easier.

128
00:12:21,808 --> 00:12:25,888
But these are exactly the kind of decisions that are going on in your agent.

129
00:12:26,048 --> 00:12:32,388
So if we've got a bunch of skills, more and more now people are starting to use large amounts of skills files.

130
00:12:32,622 --> 00:12:38,448
And remember, the whole thing that's really cool about skills is that you've got progressive disclosure in there.

131
00:12:38,702 --> 00:12:46,928
So the idea here is that if this is the actual skill file, and it might actually be a lot longer going through this, you only give the model

132
00:12:47,168 --> 00:12:55,568
this after it's decided that okay, it needs to basically use a coding write tests skill for this.

133
00:12:55,758 --> 00:12:59,168
Then you would actually go and give it this in the context window.

134
00:12:59,328 --> 00:13:11,728
So the different thing that we're doing here is that rather than just give it 50 skills in one shot, we can see for each of these 10 different categories, we've got five different skills in here.

135
00:13:11,982 --> 00:13:18,372
And if we're injecting that in the system prompt for every time we call the LLM, that's going to burn a lot of tokens.

136
00:13:18,894 --> 00:13:26,692
So the idea is that the thing that we inject into the LLM is just the categories of skills.

137
00:13:27,214 --> 00:13:36,448
And then the JEV model itself will basically take the task that we've got, it will then decide which category to pick from,

138
00:13:36,814 --> 00:13:44,548
then it will be given the list of skills for that category, and it will then choose the most appropriate one to actually do this.

139
00:13:45,006 --> 00:13:45,728
So let's look at this.

140
00:13:45,888 --> 00:13:53,764
So if the task is basically someone saying, someone got an email pretending to be our CEO asking for gift cards, is this a hacking attack?

141
00:13:54,222 --> 00:14:00,740
And you can see I'm running this through one of the bigger JEV models in here, but actually you can try it out on a lot of the different JEV models.

142
00:14:01,102 --> 00:14:10,116
You can see if I run this and route it, it goes through and it basically first decides that the category here is security and access.

143
00:14:10,606 --> 00:14:14,208
So it's done that as a choice. It's basically returned this back.

144
00:14:14,446 --> 00:14:22,916
And then in this category, it has a bunch of these in here. And we can see it decided investigate a security alert in here.

145
00:14:23,214 --> 00:14:29,808
And sure enough, the description for that is triage of suspicious login, phishing report, or security alert in there.

146
00:14:30,126 --> 00:14:40,036
So you can see in this case, what we've actually done is a sort of cascade classifier where we've done one sort of classifier first to basically pick out the class of the category,

147
00:14:40,128 --> 00:14:43,968
and then we've done a second one to actually pick out the skill in this case.

148
00:14:44,128 --> 00:14:47,908
Because we're running this locally, it's done it super quick going through this.

149
00:14:48,302 --> 00:14:56,528
All right, you can see that if I give it a sort of general question here, we've got certain things that we can actually do. Let's route it through.

150
00:14:56,974 --> 00:15:02,288
Sure enough, in this case, it's basically saying do web research, fact check a claim, right?

151
00:15:02,640 --> 00:15:11,396
It probably could have also gone for search the web, although I guess this does actually make more sense in that it's actually stating a fact and then asking if it's true here.

152
00:15:11,694 --> 00:15:17,568
And from there we could load the skill file or use any sort of API, etc., that we actually wanted to there.

153
00:15:17,806 --> 00:15:23,888
All right, one more in here you can see, okay, make me a short voiceover of this product description for a launch video.

154
00:15:24,142 --> 00:15:30,208
Now it's deciding that, okay, that relates to images, audio, and video, and it needs to be a text-to-speech skill in there.

155
00:15:30,510 --> 00:15:37,764
So this is a way of using JEV for sort of progressive disclosure with skills, especially if you've got lots of skills.

156
00:15:38,286 --> 00:15:47,008
By classifying them first into categories and then putting them into actually which skill is being chosen, you're able to get a very fast result

157
00:15:47,248 --> 00:15:47,488
here.

158
00:15:47,694 --> 00:15:56,004
And then the skill itself can actually be sent to the large language model to work out any arguments that need to go into that skill, those kinds of things.

159
00:15:56,398 --> 00:16:02,116
But this is able to help us reduce on token spend, but also just speed things up like this.

160
00:16:02,446 --> 00:16:06,628
Another one that you can use in the same sort of vein of this is doing sort of verification.

161
00:16:06,688 --> 00:16:16,368
steps of things that come back from APIs, to basically have the whole thing, make a decision of whether whatever's coming back from the API is really good and can just go

162
00:16:16,528 --> 00:16:25,408
straight through as a result, whether you need to change the arguments that you gave to the API, or whether you just need to call it again. because you got a certain type of error, etc.

163
00:16:25,838 --> 00:16:28,208
The next demo is combining this with RAG.

164
00:16:28,814 --> 00:16:35,632
So, one of the patterns in RAG that works really well is go and get a lot of information and then re-rank that information.

165
00:16:35,950 --> 00:16:41,712
And generally, there are ways to do that where you've got a separate re-ranker model. And that can work really nice as a pattern.

166
00:16:41,966 --> 00:16:49,632
The only challenge is that for certain projects, you just can't do that because of time. You can't do that because you're going to need more models.

167
00:16:49,934 --> 00:16:58,916
And especially if you're already using an embedding model on an API, you probably don't want to have lots of calls to the API for re-ranking for that kind of thing.

168
00:16:59,374 --> 00:17:02,112
So the idea here is that we can ask it a question.

169
00:17:02,352 --> 00:17:11,312
It will then go off, get a lot of different passages, then give those to the actual JEV model, which can decide,

170
00:17:11,472 --> 00:17:16,004
okay, these are the top five passages that I'm going to send to the full size LLM.

171
00:17:16,112 --> 00:17:23,152
And there are different ways you could do this. You could basically do it on a sort of yes-no thing. You could do it via sort of scoring kind of things.

172
00:17:23,312 --> 00:17:30,432
Or if you really wanted to just sort of pick one, you could actually just have a choice of one to see, okay, what's actually going to go through in there.

173
00:17:30,798 --> 00:17:35,044
Now for me playing around with this, I find the scoring one seems to work quite nicely here.

174
00:17:35,278 --> 00:17:40,752
We then basically get a score. So in our question here is why are my webhook requests failing with an error?

175
00:17:41,006 --> 00:17:50,432
And then we can see sure enough, we're getting back webhooks, verifying signatures, second up webhooks raw bodies, third up was proxies, fourth up was webhooks

176
00:17:50,592 --> 00:17:51,364
signing secrets.

177
00:17:51,472 --> 00:17:55,812
And you can see that we've got the five things now that we can basically send back.

178
00:17:56,238 --> 00:17:59,792
And this is something that you could even use if you weren't using an embedding model.

179
00:17:59,982 --> 00:18:07,908
